{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Sweet.</b> It is known that the rich man fears day and night for his wealth — from the king, from robbers, and from thieves. And the matter of 'the satiety of abundance of wealth' is that at night he thinks thoughts how his soul may be sated by wealth, or it may be 'sated' in the sense of turning away from food, as in the matter of 'lest he sate him and he vomit' (Proverbs 25:16). And <b>the sleep of the laborer</b> is vocalized as pathach, like every word in construct state; and if I give <b>his sleep</b> with qamatz — for the lamed has cut off the construct relation — this is from Rabbi Moses ha-Kohen the Sephardi, of blessed memory: that the meaning of qamatz <b>his sleep</b> is 'my sleep,' and likewise <b>'also my pleasant inheritance is upon me, O my strength'</b> — these too are with qamatz."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "Reading ואם אתן שנת לעיני קמוץ as 'if I give [it as] shenat with qamatz before [a following word]'; the argument turns on whether the qamatz under lamed marks construct (pathach) or absolute (qamatz gadol) vocalization. The cited phrase from Judges 5:30 is paraphrased; its precise form here may reflect a variant or paraphrastic citation."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:27:50.003098209Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}